{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}Switching users{% endblock %}

{% block body %}
    <h1>Switching users</h1>
    <p>
        Impersonation is Security's, and stays Security's. What changes with the bundle registered
        is who answers the <em>authorization</em> half: <code>is_granted()</code> and
        <code>is_granted_for_user()</code> become the component's, while
        <code>impersonation_path()</code>, <code>impersonation_exit_path()</code> and
        <code>access_decision()</code> keep being delegated —
        <code>SecurityExtensionWithoutAuthorization</code> is the seam.
    </p>

    {% if is_granted('ROLE_ALLOWED_TO_SWITCH') %}
        <h2>Switch in</h2>
        <p>
            <a href="{{ impersonation_path('user') }}">Impersonate “user”</a> ·
            <a href="{{ impersonation_path('manager') }}">Impersonate “manager”</a>
        </p>
        <p class="muted">
            Both links are built by <code>impersonation_path()</code> for the current route.
            <code>ROLE_ALLOWED_TO_SWITCH</code> is not held by anyone directly — the admin account
            reaches it through the role hierarchy, which the component reads from
            <code>security.role_hierarchy</code>.
        </p>
    {% elseif is_granted('IS_IMPERSONATOR') %}
        <p class="flash flash-success">
            You are impersonating <strong>{{ app.user.userIdentifier }}</strong>.
            <a href="{{ impersonation_exit_path() }}">Stop impersonating</a>
        </p>
        <p class="muted">
            While this lasts, <a href="{{ path('app_playground_impersonation') }}">the page guarded
            by <code>IS_IMPERSONATOR</code></a> is reachable, and at no other time.
        </p>
    {% else %}
        <p class="muted">
            Sign in as <code>admin</code> to switch users from here: switching in takes
            <code>ROLE_ALLOWED_TO_SWITCH</code>, which only the admin account reaches.
        </p>
    {% endif %}

    <h2>Two kinds of "on behalf of"</h2>
    <p class="muted">
        Impersonation replaces the requester: while it lasts, the app sees the impersonated user,
        and <code>IS_IMPERSONATOR</code> is what betrays the substitution.
        <a href="{{ path('app_playground_on_behalf') }}">Delegation</a> is the other shape and the
        component's own — the requester is not replaced, an actor is named behind it, and a rule can
        ask about either one. Neither needs a firewall in the second case.
    </p>
{% endblock %}
